---
/**
 * A1 — volumetric baseline.
 *
 * The exhibit is A1-01, the seeded-defect positive control. Its data table is built from
 * the same block of the case summary that the figure was drawn from, republished under
 * site/public/data/ and checked against its digest during this build.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Figure from "../../../components/Figure.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/DataTable.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
import { checkedExhibit } from "../../../scripts/exhibits";
import { download, readJson } from "../../../scripts/public-data";
import { NAV, NOT_RUN } from "../../../scripts/site";
import { href } from "../../../lib/urls";

const a1 = await checkedExhibit("A1-01");
const csv = await download("a1_01_defect_sensitivity.csv");
const json = await download("a1_01_defect_sensitivity.json");

interface D1 {
  description: string;
  gate_used_for_detection: number;
  cases: Array<{
    name: string;
    defect_class: string;
    magnitude: string;
    recovery_relative_error: number;
    detected_at_A1_gate: boolean;
  }>;
}
const d1 = await readJson<D1>("a1_01_defect_sensitivity.json");

const rows = d1.cases.map((c) => ({
  name: c.name.replace(/_/g, " "),
  klass: c.defect_class,
  mag: c.magnitude,
  err:
    c.recovery_relative_error === 0
      ? "0, exactly"
      : c.recovery_relative_error.toExponential(3),
  seen: c.detected_at_A1_gate ? "yes" : "no",
}));

const visible = d1.cases.filter((c) => c.detected_at_A1_gate).length;

const contents = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "evidence", label: "Evidence" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What this does not establish" },
  { id: "reproduce", label: "Reproduce and download" },
];

const description =
  "A1 recovers gas in place from a noise-free volumetric history to machine precision, and a seeded-defect control shows how narrow that exactness is.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="A1 — volumetric baseline"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/studies/a1/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Study A1 — supporting work</p>
  <h1>The exact recovery, and how narrow "exact" is</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    On data generated by the volumetric gas material balance with no measurement error, the
    p/Z inverse recovers gas in place with a relative error of <strong>exactly zero</strong>
    {" "}at constant deviation factor, and <strong>+5.40e-14</strong> with a pressure-dependent
    one, against a tolerance of 2.0e-11 derived from the conditioning of the extrapolation
    before the run. What that establishes is a property of the code, and a narrower property
    than the zero suggests.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Synthetic throughout.</strong> {NOT_RUN.fieldData}
  </p>

  <section id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-h">
    <h2 id="overview-h">Overview</h2>

    <p>
      A closed tank of 100 Bscf at 4000 psia and 640 degR, produced at a constant 2.5e7
      scf/day and observed at eleven equally spaced times. The forward generator solves the
      implicit balance at each step and reports the pressure and the deviation factor
      separately; the inverse never sees G — it sees eleven pairs of cumulative production
      and p/Z and reports the x-intercept of the least-squares line through them. The two
      modules share no code.
    </p>

    <p>
      That separation buys less than it sounds. The standard-condition group and the
      reservoir temperature cancel identically out of the balance, so a wrong standard-volume
      basis cannot show up here at all — and the diagnostic below confirms it by corrupting
      that basis by 50 percent and recovering G with a relative error of exactly zero.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="note" title="One of the four statements, and only one">
      <p>
        This study reaches <strong>numerical verification</strong>: the estimator inverts the
        forward map to the precision the arithmetic allows. It does not attempt a
        conditional pressure reconstruction, it establishes nothing about what a reservoir is
        actually doing, and it establishes nothing about whether a number is fit to carry a
        decision. The generator and the estimator assume the same physics, so this study is
        structurally incapable of catching a wrong assumption.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>The number the study says is worth remembering</h3>
    <p>
      It is not the exact recovery. It is the <strong>−5.74 percent</strong> error the same
      estimator produces, at R-squared <strong>0.99707</strong>, when the analyst reuses the
      initial deviation factor instead of the pressure-dependent one — an R-squared that
      reads as an excellent fit while the answer is 5.7 Bscf short. The reported standard
      error of 1.36 percent does not contain that error: the estimate sits 4.5 standard
      errors from the truth, because the standard error describes scatter and this is bias.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sign of that bias does not generalise. It is negative because the deviation factor
      falls below its initial value across this particular depletion window; moving the
      window below the minimum of Z(p), at about 2347 psia, flips it positive — +0.61 percent
      at an initial pressure of 3000 psia, +4.69 percent at 2000 psia. The pre-registered
      acceptance criterion that predicted a negative bias between 1 and 10 percent is a
      correct prediction about one configuration and was not evidence about the class. The
      consistent fit, by contrast, recovers G to better than 1.5e-13 in all eight windows
      tested, so <em>that</em> exactness does generalise.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-h">
    <h2 id="evidence-h">Evidence</h2>

    <Figure
      id={a1.id}
      number={a1.number}
      title={a1.title}
      svg={a1.svg}
      units={a1.units}
      interpretation={`Of ${d1.cases.length} seeded defects, ${visible} move the recovered inventory past the study's own detection gate and the rest leave it exactly unchanged; the invisible class includes a 50 percent error in the standard-volume basis.`}
      caveat={a1.caveat}
      longDescription={a1.longDescription}
      evidenceClass={a1.evidenceClass}
      tableLabel="Data table — the seeded defects"
      tableOpen={true}
      svgDownload={a1.svgDownload}
      dataDownload={json.path}
      dataDownloadLabel={json.name}
    >
      <DataTable
        caption={`${d1.description}. A defect counts as visible when it moves the recovery error past ${d1.gate_used_for_detection.toExponential(1)} relative.`}
        captionTitle="Table A1-01"
        columns={[
          { key: "name", label: "Seeded defect", rowHeader: true },
          { key: "klass", label: "Class" },
          { key: "mag", label: "Magnitude" },
          { key: "err", label: "Recovery error", unit: "relative", numeric: true },
          { key: "seen", label: "Visible at the gate" },
        ]}
        rows={rows}
        id="t-a1-01"
      />
      <Fragment slot="footnote">
        <p>
          Three classes, three different answers. <strong>Uniform multiplicative error in the
          ordinate is invisible at any magnitude probed</strong> — the x-intercept of a
          straight line does not move when the ordinate is rescaled, so a wrong
          standard-volume basis, a wrong reservoir temperature and an analyst dividing by the
          wrong constant deviation factor all recover G exactly. <strong>Multiplicative error
          in the abscissa passes straight through</strong>, one for one. <strong>A point-wise
          nudge of 0.01 psia on a single observation is visible</strong>, and where it sits
          along the history decides the sign.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </Figure>

    <Callout kind="caveat" title="This is a positive control, not a result about the physics">
      <p>
        The diagnostic is post-hoc: it was added after the pre-registered experiments, it is
        not gated, and it changed no threshold and no result. It exists to answer a question
        the zeros above could not — which defects the design can see — and it is evidence
        about the instrument, not about a reservoir.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
    <h2 id="limits-h">What this study does not establish</h2>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="The checked arithmetic excludes a whole class">
      <p>
        Any uniform multiplicative error in the ordinate, the standard-volume basis included,
        is invisible here at any magnitude probed. The exact recovery is evidence only
        against the defect classes the seeded-defect control shows are visible.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="The generator and the estimator assume the same physics">
      <p>
        So this study cannot catch a wrong assumption, only a wrong implementation. Catching a
        wrong assumption is what study
        {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a4/")}>A4</a> was built to do, by generating a history
        from a model the estimator does not contain.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="The kindest possible error structure, and one input never switched on">
      <p>
        The noise model is independent Gaussian, which is the kindest error structure a
        pressure survey can have. The one input that maps one for one into the answer — error
        in cumulative production — is never switched on at all. The study rests on a single
        design: eleven evenly spaced points, one gas gravity, one temperature.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="Acceptance bands without derivations">
      <p>
        One gate is wider than its own derivation without a stated reason, and two others
        have no derivation at all. The study records that rather than tightening them after
        the fact.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="Determinism is machine-local">
      <p>
        The run summary is byte-identical across repeated executions on one machine and one
        interpreter build — CPython 3.13.2, macOS on arm64. That is a determinism result for
        that environment and is not a claim about any other. {NOT_RUN.hostedCi}
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="reproduce" aria-labelledby="reproduce-h">
    <h2 id="reproduce-h">Reproduce, and download the numbers</h2>

    <pre><code>PYTHONPATH=src python3 cases/A1_volumetric_baseline/run.py \
    --out artifacts/A1_volumetric_baseline/run-008</code></pre>

    <p>
      Python standard library only. The shipped run is <code>run-006</code>, whose committed
      snapshot this site's figure was drawn from; the snapshot's digest is recorded in the
      generated figure manifest and re-checked when the figures are rendered.
    </p>

    <ul class="downloads">
      {[csv, json].map((d) => (
        <li>
          <a href={d.href} download>
            {d.name}
          </a>
          <span class="downloads__meta">
            {d.size} — {d.description}
          </span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p>
      Both files carry the full seeded-defect block; there is no per-selection download on
      this page because there is no selector on it. The
      {" "}<a href={href("/methods/")}>methods page</a> lists the verification profiles and
      the digests.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .downloads {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .downloads li {
      padding-block: var(--space-2);
      border-block-end: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
    }
    .downloads__meta {
      display: block;
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--c-ink-2);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
